1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,412
How Plants Communicate.

2
00:00:02,362 --> 00:00:08,325
Plants communicate through chemical, electrical, and fungal-mediated signaling networks.

3
00:00:08,325 --> 00:00:19,950
When herbivores damage plant tissues, wounded plants release volatile organic compounds (VOCs) — including green leaf volatiles, terpenoids, and methyl jasmonate — within minutes.

4
00:00:19,950 --> 00:00:32,812
These airborne signals travel up to several meters, triggering defense responses in neighboring undamaged plants: production of proteinase inhibitors, alkaloids, and extrafloral nectar that attracts predatory insects.

5
00:00:32,812 --> 00:00:40,600
Lima bean plants exposed to volatiles from beetle-damaged neighbors showed 40 percent greater trichome density compared to controls.

6
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:48,400
Below ground, an estimated 90 percent of land plants form mutualistic associations with arbuscular mycorrhizal fungi.

7
00:00:48,400 --> 00:01:03,412
These fungi colonize root cortical cells and extend hyphae through the soil, increasing nutrient absorption area by up to 100-fold in exchange for photosynthetic carbon — plants allocate 4 to 20 percent of fixed carbon to mycorrhizal partners.

8
00:01:03,412 --> 00:01:12,100
These fungal networks, termed the “wood wide web,” interconnect multiple plants of the same and different species within a single mycelial network.

9
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:23,350
Carbon isotope tracing studies at the University of Basel showed that shade-stressed Douglas fir seedlings received 4 to 10 percent of their carbon from neighboring mature trees via mycorrhizal connections.

10
00:01:23,350 --> 00:01:26,425
Plants also use electrical signaling.

11
00:01:26,425 --> 00:01:37,475
When caterpillar feeding damages a leaf, action potentials propagate through the phloem at rates of 1 to 5 millimeters per second, triggering systemic jasmonic acid production throughout the plant.

12
00:01:37,475 --> 00:01:43,700
The Venus flytrap closes its trap via two successive action potentials within 20 seconds.

13
00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:58,700
These discoveries have redefined plant biology, demonstrating that plants sense, process, and respond to environmental information analogously to neural systems, operating on timescales 10 to 10,000 times slower than animal nervous systems.

